<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Siege</p><h1 class="enc-title">Siege of Calvi</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1794">1794</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-wars">French Revolutionary Wars</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Siege of Calvi was fought from June to August 1794, when British forces and Corsican allies besieged the French garrison of Calvi in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a>. Captain <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horatio-nelson">Horatio Nelson</a> commanded naval guns ashore and lost the sight of his right eye from flying debris. The French surrender completed the expulsion of French forces from the island and secured the Anglo-Corsican Kingdom.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/siege-of-calvi.jpg" alt="Siege of Calvi" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bowyer, Robert, Worthington, William Henry &amp; Bromley, William, 1808 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nelson_at_Calvi_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-convention">National Convention</a> ordered the arrest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pasquale-paoli">Pasquale Paoli</a> in 1793, he raised an irregular army, drove the French garrison into three fortified northern ports and asked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> for help. Admiral Lord Hood's Mediterranean Fleet, delayed by the Siege of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulon">Toulon</a>, agreed to support him in return for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/corsica">Corsica</a> becoming a self-governing part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a>. A British force took San Fiorenzo in February 1794 and starved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bastia">Bastia</a> into surrender by May, leaving Calvi, where the pro-revolutionary Corsican Raphaël de Casabianca commanded, as the last French stronghold.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Calvi was covered by two modern forts, Fort Mozello to the west and Fort Mollinochesco to the south-west, and by the frigates Melpomène and Mignonne in the bay. Wishing to finish before the malaria season, General Charles Stuart landed at the cove of Port-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agra">Agra</a>, 3 miles from the town, with Nelson's squadron led by HMS Agamemnon. Roads had to be built to haul the guns up the mountains, and the batteries were not all in place until 4 July.</p><p>Fort Mollinochesco was so damaged by 6 July that its garrison withdrew into the town, and the frigates retreated into the harbour. Fort Mozello was then bombarded for twelve days; Captain Walter Serocold was killed at a battery and Nelson was hit by stone splinters on 12 July. On 18 July the 7th Foot under Lieutenant-colonel John Moore and the 18th Foot stormed the breach. Casabianca answered a summons with the town's motto, &quot;Calvi is always loyal&quot;, but short of ammunition he agreed to a truce and capitulated on 10 August, after 51 days of siege.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>British battle casualties were 30 killed and 58 wounded, but malaria and dysentery had left only 400 men fit for duty, which explains the generous terms: 300 French soldiers and 247 Corsican supporters were given passage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. Austria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> protested, and Hood accused Stuart of &quot;great tenderness&quot; for refusing to bombard French hospitals. The British took 113 guns and the frigate Melpomène, which joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a>. Corsica passed under the viceroy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilbert-elliot-1st-earl-of-minto">Sir Gilbert Elliot</a>, but quarrels between Elliot, Paoli and Stuart weakened British rule until the withdrawal at the end of 1796.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Siege of Calvi” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1794, with British naval help, Corsican forces drove the French from the island; Nelson lost the sight of an eye at the <b>siege of Calvi</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/anglo-corsican-kingdom">Anglo-Corsican Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
